# Balls and Buckets — 3D Physics Animation

A 3D modelling and animation project created in Blender, exploring rigid body physics, object interaction and camera movement. The project demonstrates how physics-based animation can be combined with visual design to create a dynamic 3D scene.

## Project Overview

The animation features colourful balls falling from an upper bucket into a second bucket below. The movement is generated using Blender's rigid body physics system, creating a natural interaction between the objects.

## Key Features

- **3D Modelling:** Created the buckets and spherical objects using basic modelling techniques.
- **Physics Simulation:** Applied rigid body physics to simulate realistic falling and collision behaviour.
- **Materials & Lighting:** Designed materials and configured lighting to enhance the visual presentation.
- **Camera Animation:** Used camera tracking with an Empty object to create smooth camera movement.
- **Audio Integration:** Added background music using Blender's Video Sequencer.
- **Scene Organisation:** Organised objects into collections and maintained incremental project versions throughout development.

## Tools & Technologies

- Blender
- 3D Modelling
- Rigid Body Physics
- Camera Tracking
- Materials & Lighting
- Animation & Rendering

## Project Challenges

One of the main challenges was creating a seamless animation loop using baked rigid body physics. Although a fully seamless loop was not achieved, the final animation successfully demonstrates the intended physical interaction.

